    "title": "Is there a launch today? NASA, SpaceX, Blue Origin launch schedule in Florida",
    "text": "April 1, 2026Updated April 16, 2026, 10:56 a.m. ETFlorida's Space Coast hosted a record-shattering 109 orbital rocket launches during 2025, soaring beyond all previous annual records.Will this year's final total surpass that lofty sum from Cape Canaveral Space Force Station and NASA's Kennedy Space Center? By the end of March, 22 rockets had taken flight from the Sunshine State.Looking ahead, the Cape's April launch schedule will feature the usual heavy diet of SpaceX Starlink satellite missions — with space fans anticipating Blue Origin's next New Glenn rocket launch.Following are the latest missions coming up from the Cape. All launches are listed in Eastern Daylight Time. But be aware: Dates and times routinely change for a wide variety of reasons.FLORIDA TODAY's Space Team provides live blog coverage of each launch at floridatoday.com/space, starting at least 90 minutes before liftoff. For questions or comments, email Space Reporter Rick Neale at Rneale@floridatoday.com. No earlier than Sunday, April 19: Blue Origin New Glenn-3Mission:A Blue Origin New Glenn rocket will launch AST SpaceMobile’s next-generation Block 2 BlueBird satellite to low-Earth orbit, a Federal Aviation Administration operations plan advisory shows.Launch window: 6:45 a.m. to 12:19 p.m.Trajectory: TBA.Location: Launch Complex 36 at Cape Canaveral Space Force Station.Sonic booms: No.Live FLORIDA TODAY Space Team coverage: Starts two hours before liftoff at floridatoday.com/space.Monday, April 20: SpaceX GPS III-8Mission: A SpaceX Falcon 9 rocket will launch a GPS III satellite for the Space Force on a national security mission, a Federal Aviation Administration operations plan advisory shows.Launch window: 2:48 a.m. to 4:05 a.m.Trajectory: TBA.Location: Launch Complex 40 at Cape Canaveral Space Force Station.Sonic booms: TBALive FLORIDA TODAY Space Team coverage: Starts 90 minutes before liftoff at floridatoday.com/space.Monday, April 27: United Launch Alliance Amazon Leo 6Mission: A ULA Atlas V rocket with five solid rocket boosters will launch the next batch of 29 Amazon Leo broadband satellites.Launch: TBA.Trajectory: TBA.Location: Launch Complex 41 at Cape Canaveral Space Force Station.Sonic booms: No.Live FLORIDA TODAY Space Team coverage: Starts 90 minutes before liftoff at floridatoday.com/space.Late April: SpaceX ViaSat-3 F3Mission: A mighty SpaceX Falcon Heavy rocket will launch a Boeing-built ViaSat-3 satellite to provide connectivity to the Asia-Pacific region.Launch window: TBA.Trajectory: TBA.Location: Pad 39A at NASA's Kennedy Space Center.Sonic booms: TBA.Live FLORIDA TODAY Space Team coverage: Starts 90 minutes before liftoff at floridatoday.com/space.First half of 2026: Blue Origin Blue Moon PathfinderMission: A Blue Origin New Glenn rocket will launch a Blue Moon lunar lander carrying NASA payloads on a demonstration mission.Launch window: TBA.Trajectory: TBA.Location: Launch Complex 36 at Cape Canaveral Space Force Station.Sonic booms: No.Live FLORIDA TODAY Space Team coverage: Starts two hours before liftoff at floridatoday.com/space.No earlier than April: NASA Boeing Starliner-1Mission: An uncrewed Boeing Starliner will deliver cargo to the International Space Station and undergo in-flight validation of a series of system upgrades.Launch window: TBA.Trajectory: TBA.Location: TBA.Sonic booms: TBA.Live FLORIDA TODAY Space Team coverage: Starts two hours before liftoff at floridatoday.com/space.Fourth quarter 2026: ULA Sierra Space Dream ChaserMission: Sierra Space will launch its uncrewed Dream Chaser space plane atop a ULA Vulcan rocket from Cape Canaveral Space Force Station.Launch window: TBA.Location: Launch Complex 41.Live FLORIDA TODAY Space Team coverage: Starts two hours minutes before liftoff at floridatoday.com/space.For the latest news and launch schedule from Cape Canaveral Space Force Station and NASA's Kennedy Space Center, visit floridatoday.com/space. Another easy way: Click here to sign up for our weekly Space newsletter.Space is important to us and that's why we're working to bring you top coverage of the industry and Florida launches. Journalism like this takes time and resources. Please support it with a subscription here.",
